      "type": "drye-section-06-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "faq_item_P8tAUf": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Does it work under nitrile gloves specifically?",
            "answer": "Nitrile, latex and vinyl. DRYE is made to be worn under sealed gloves. It is 0.4 mm thin, so it fits under standard gloves without changing your outer glove."
          }
        },
        "faq_item_MawBHd": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Will my hands get hotter?",
            "answer": "Any extra layer can add warmth.\n\nBut sealed gloves already trap humidity fast. What usually feels hot is wet skin-to-glove contact.\n\nDRYE adds a thin 0.4 mm textile layer next to your skin. The glove can still feel humid inside, but your skin stays dry — so most users describe it as more breathable, not like wearing a thermal liner."
          }
        },
        "faq_item_7mHBzU": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What about HEMA & Acetone?",
            "answer": "DRYE isn’t a chemical barrier — and neither is a thin nitrile glove.\n\nTesting on real nail products found thin nitrile held up to nail gel for about 5 minutes. DRYE doesn’t stop HEMA or prevent sensitization, and nobody should tell you a liner can.\n\nWhat it does add is a practical layer between your skin and the glove. It reduces wet skin-to-glove contact and keeps the skin side drier during long wear. Many nail techs rotate 2–3 DRYE pairs during the day so they always put on a fresh, dry liner between services."
          }
        },
        "faq_item_3kyJxA": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What if I have long or sharp nails?",
            "answer": "DRYE is made from a thin 0.4 mm textile, so very long or sharp nails can snag the fabric if you pull too hard.\n\nPut the liner on gently, keep your nail tips smooth, and avoid forcing the fingertips. Once it’s on, your outer glove goes over DRYE like normal."
          }
        },
        "faq_item_JpnHYA": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What if it doesn't work for me?",
            "answer": "You have 30 days to try it. If it doesn’t improve your shift, you get a refund."
          }
        },
        "faq_item_zB3hk8": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I wash it? How?",
            "answer": "Machine wash up to 90°C / 194°F. Hundreds of washes. Air dry is best — it's thin enough to dry almost instantly, and skipping the dryer prevents pilling. Most techs keep two or three pairs and swap at lunch."
          }
        },
        "faq_item_aWcXAV": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How long does it last? ",
            "answer": "With regular use and washing, most users should expect 6–12 months. The function is built into the textile structure, not a coating that washes out."
          }
        },
        "faq_item_P863DU": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "disabled": true,
          "settings": {
            "question": "Who it’s not for ",
            "answer": "DRYE works best when gloves stay on for longer periods. If you remove gloves every few minutes, the liner may not have enough time to help. Non-sterile use only."
          }
        }
      },
